SUMMARY:UCLA Open Source Meetup: Keeping the World on Time
LOCATION:Charles E. Young Research Library\, Room 11630L
DESCRIPTION:Join colleagues from UCLA and across the University of Califor
 nia for a hybrid lunch-hour conversation with UCLA Computer Science Teachi
 ng Professor Paul Eggert\, primary coordinator of the public-domain Time Z
 one Database (TZDB) and a longtime contributor to GNU software projects.\n
 \nTZDB does far more than keep the clocks on our phones and computers accu
 rate. It supports timestamps on documents and photographs\, preserves hist
 orical time-zone information used by software\, and must respond when gove
 rnments change their clocks with little warning.\n\nIn “Keeping the Worl
 d on Time: Inside the Time Zone Database\,” Eggert will introduce this w
 idely used but largely invisible infrastructure and discuss the technical\
 , legal\, political and collaborative challenges involved in sustaining it
 . The program will feature a 30–40-minute talk followed by an extended Q
 &A and conversation with the audience.\n\nAttend In Person or Online\n\nTh
 is is a hybrid event. Advance registration is required for both attendance
  options. Choose In Person or Online when registering.\n\nIn person: Join 
 us at the UCLA Library Data Science Center in Charles E. Young Research Li
 brary\, Room 11630L. Seating is limited to 16 people. Bring your own lunch
 \; light refreshments and beverages will be provided.\nOnline: Join the li
 ve program and Q&A via Zoom from UCLA or another UC campus. Online registr
 ants will receive the Zoom access information by email.\n\nFeatured Speake
 r: Prof. Paul Eggert\n\nPaul Eggert is the primary coordinator of the publ
 ic-domain Time Zone Database (TZDB)\, which coordinates timestamps on comp
 uting devices worldwide\, and a copy of which surely resides on your smart
 phone. He designed TZDB's naming standard and continues to manage its data
  and code amid technical\, legal\, and political challenges. Over the year
 s he has also contributed to core free software projects\, including the G
 NU C Compiler\, C Library\, Coreutils\, Diffutils\, Gnulib\, and Emacs\, a
 nd in 2022 he was given the Free Software Foundation Award for the Advance
 ment of Free Software. After a career in the software industry that includ
 ed positions at the world's first independent computer software company\, 
 the world's then-second largest computer company\, and startups\, he joine
 d the UCLA Computer Science Department\, where he is now a Teaching Profes
 sor.\n\nWho Should Attend?\n\nThis event is open to students\, faculty\, s
 taff\, researchers\, librarians and technologists from UCLA and across the
  UC system. All experience levels are welcome\, from people curious about 
 open source to active contributors and project maintainers.\n\nHosted by t
 he UCLA OSPO in partnership with the UC Open Source Program Office (OSPO) 
 Network.\n\nLearn More About the Time Zone Database\n\nFor an accessible i
 ntroduction to Eggert and his work\, read the UCLA Samueli profile “Time
  Zone King: How One UCLA Computer Scientist Keeps Digital Clocks Ticking.
 ” You can also visit the official IANA Time Zone Database website or exp
 lore the TZDB source code and project documentation on GitHub.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.library.ucla.edu/event/17080330
